============================================================================
STATEMENT 3: InsertProductAsync() - Transaction with SCOPE_IDENTITY
============================================================================

ORIGINAL SQL SERVER STATEMENT:
DECLARE @NewProductId INT;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
    VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity);
    
    SET @NewProductId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
    
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (@NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, GETDATE());
    
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
        LastUpdated = GETDATE()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
COMMIT;

SELECT @NewProductId;

DMS TOOL OUTPUT:
Status: error
Error: Metadata model creation failed: {'error': 'Metadata model creation did not complete after 15 attempts'}
Timestamp: 2026-02-01T12:42:25.020469

MANUAL CONVERSION REASONING:
- DECLARE @variable INT -> PostgreSQL uses DO blocks or removes DECLARE for simple cases
- BEGIN TRANSACTION -> BEGIN (PostgreSQL syntax)
- SCOPE_IDENTITY() -> Use RETURNING clause in INSERT statement
- GETDATE() -> NOW() or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
- SET @variable = value -> PostgreSQL variable assignment in DO block
- COMMIT -> COMMIT (same)
- For ADO.NET usage, restructure to use RETURNING clause instead of SCOPE_IDENTITY()

POSTGRESQL CONVERTED STATEMENT (Option 1 - Using RETURNING for ADO.NET):
-- This approach uses RETURNING which is more efficient for ADO.NET
INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity)
RETURNING ProductId;

-- Note: The transaction will be managed at the ADO.NET level using NpgsqlTransaction
-- The ProductHistory and ProductStats updates will need to be separate statements
-- executed within the same transaction scope

POSTGRESQL CONVERTED STATEMENT (Option 2 - Full Transaction Block):
DO $$
DECLARE NewProductId INT;
BEGIN
    INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
    VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity)
    RETURNING ProductId INTO NewProductId;
    
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, NOW());
    
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
        LastUpdated = NOW()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
    
    -- Return the new product ID
    RAISE NOTICE 'NewProductId: %', NewProductId;
END $$;

BEST APPROACH FOR ADO.NET:
Break the transaction into separate statements managed by NpgsqlTransaction:
1. INSERT with RETURNING clause to get new ProductId
2. INSERT into ProductHistory using the returned ProductId
3. UPDATE ProductStats

============================================================================
STATEMENT 4: UpdateProductAsync() - Transaction with DECLARE
============================================================================

ORIGINAL SQL SERVER STATEMENT:
BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    DECLARE @OldPrice DECIMAL(18,2);
    DECLARE @OldStock INT;
    
    SELECT @OldPrice = Price, @OldStock = StockQuantity
    FROM Products
    WHERE ProductId = @ProductId;
    
    UPDATE Products
    SET 
        Name = @Name,
        Description = @Description,
        Price = @Price,
        StockQuantity = @StockQuantity,
        ModifiedDate = GETDATE()
    WHERE ProductId = @ProductId;
    
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (@ProductId, 'UPDATE', @OldPrice, @Price, @OldStock, @StockQuantity, GETDATE());
    
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts - @OldPrice + @Price) / TotalProducts,
        LastUpdated = GETDATE()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
COMMIT;

DMS TOOL OUTPUT:
Status: error (inferred from pattern)

MANUAL CONVERSION REASONING:
- BEGIN TRANSACTION -> Managed by NpgsqlTransaction in ADO.NET
- DECLARE @variable TYPE -> Use WITH clause or inline subqueries in PostgreSQL
- SELECT @variable = column -> Use CTE or subquery for better PostgreSQL compatibility
- GETDATE() -> NOW() or CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
- Transaction structure should be managed at ADO.NET level

POSTGRESQL CONVERTED STATEMENT:
WITH OldValues AS (
    SELECT Price as OldPrice, StockQuantity as OldStock
    FROM Products
    WHERE ProductId = @ProductId
)
UPDATE Products
SET 
    Name = @Name,
    Description = @Description,
    Price = @Price,
    StockQuantity = @StockQuantity,
    ModifiedDate = NOW()
WHERE ProductId = @ProductId;

INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
SELECT @ProductId, 'UPDATE', OldPrice, @Price, OldStock, @StockQuantity, NOW()
FROM Products p, (SELECT Price as OldPrice, StockQuantity as OldStock FROM Products WHERE ProductId = @ProductId) old_vals
WHERE p.ProductId = @ProductId
LIMIT 1;

UPDATE ProductStats
SET 
    AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts - old.Price + @Price) / TotalProducts,
    LastUpdated = NOW()
FROM (SELECT Price FROM Products WHERE ProductId = @ProductId) old
WHERE StatId = 1;

SIMPLER APPROACH FOR ADO.NET (Recommended):
Execute as separate statements within NpgsqlTransaction:
1. SELECT to get old values
2. UPDATE Products with NOW()
3. INSERT into ProductHistory with old values
4. UPDATE ProductStats

